1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,200
This session develops the advanced respiratory skills in the school
reading table. The diagnostic lecture explained gases; now decide what
the patient actually needs before the next laboratory result arrives.

2
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:27,072
Connect mechanism to a practical action: open and maintain the airway,
improve effective airflow, support gas exchange and protect
circulation. A better oxygen display is only one observation.

3
00:00:27,072 --> 00:00:37,320
We will rehearse bronchodilator delivery, the full CPAP screen, PEEP
during assisted ventilation and the decision to stop an ineffective or
unsafe treatment.

4
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:49,368
Use the named local procedure and the actual equipment instructions
together. The reading provides source detail; physical assembly and
competence still require the school device and instructor.

5
00:00:49,368 --> 00:01:00,576
Assess patency and the ability to maintain and protect the airway.
Speech, secretions, vomit and responsiveness can immediately change
the support options.

6
00:01:00,576 --> 00:01:12,264
Look at rate and depth together, listen for useful air entry and
assess effort. A very rapid shallow pattern may move little effective
air despite looking busy.

7
00:01:12,264 --> 00:01:22,224
Check circulation while respiratory care proceeds. Positive pressure,
medication and the underlying illness may all affect pressure and
perfusion.

8
00:01:22,224 --> 00:01:33,672
Treat immediate threats, request appropriate help and plan conveyance
without making the distressed patient walk. Do not finish a long
history before providing necessary support.

9
00:01:33,672 --> 00:01:45,840
Respiratory distress describes increased work and difficulty.
Respiratory failure means the required oxygenation or ventilation is
not being maintained; the two can overlap.

10
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:56,712
A tiring patient may become less noisy and less active because effort
is failing. Falling rate and a quieter chest are not automatically
signs of recovery.

11
00:01:56,712 --> 00:02:09,456
Look for worsening responsiveness, shallow movement, poor air entry
and deteriorating perfusion. These can demand assisted ventilation
even if supplemental oxygen has improved saturation.

12
00:02:09,456 --> 00:02:19,992
Reassess after every meaningful change. A patient who initially
qualified for CPAP can leave that branch when ventilation or airway
protection deteriorates.

13
00:02:19,992 --> 00:02:32,040
Obstructed airways increase resistance and make expiration difficult.
Bronchospasm, edema, mucus and lost elastic support can contribute in
different proportions.

14
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:43,728
Pulmonary edema adds fluid to the gas-exchange region and increases
work. Positive pressure can help a selected patient, but the
circulation still needs careful assessment.

15
00:02:43,728 --> 00:02:56,904
Embolism, pneumonia, pneumothorax, anemia and metabolic acidosis can
also present with breathlessness. Neither wheeze nor crackles
identifies one disease by itself.

16
00:02:56,904 --> 00:03:06,528
State the leading mechanism and a dangerous alternative. This keeps
treatment patient-specific when cardiac and respiratory disease
coexist.

17
00:03:06,528 --> 00:03:18,288
Asthma combines variable bronchoconstriction with inflammation and
mucus. A bronchodilator may quickly address one component without
instantly clearing all obstruction.

18
00:03:18,288 --> 00:03:29,832
Expiration becomes prolonged and the next inspiration can begin before
emptying is complete. Explain this mechanism instead of merely
memorizing an increased-work label.

19
00:03:29,832 --> 00:03:39,648
Wheeze requires moving air. A severe presentation with a silent chest
or barely audible air entry can be more dangerous than a loud wheeze.

20
00:03:39,648 --> 00:03:51,912
Use brief assessment, prompt appropriate treatment and repeated
functional reassessment. Do not assume every ambulance asthma call has
the same severity or a proven status diagnosis.

21
00:03:51,912 --> 00:04:05,184
Prior intensive care, intubation or repeated severe exacerbations
raise concern. Ask these questions early without requiring a
breathless patient to recite a complete medical biography.

22
00:04:05,184 --> 00:04:15,216
Find out how much rescue medicine was used and whether it helped.
Increasing use with shrinking benefit is more useful than the name of
an inhaler alone.

23
00:04:15,216 --> 00:04:26,520
Ask about fever, exposures, empty devices and barriers to treatment.
Use neutral language rather than assuming missed treatment reflects
rebellion or indifference.

24
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:36,312
Obtain relevant action-plan and treatment-limit information when
available. These inform the pathway but do not replace assessment of
the current threat.

25
00:04:36,312 --> 00:04:48,720
Emphysematous destruction reduces elastic recoil and gas-exchange
surface. Chronic bronchitic disease adds inflammation and mucus; many
patients have overlapping features.

26
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:58,608
A new stressor acts on limited reserve. Infection is common, but a
cardiac event, pneumothorax or equipment problem may be contributing.

27
00:04:58,608 --> 00:05:10,200
Rapid breathing does not exclude dangerous hypercapnia. Small
ineffective breaths, dead space, obstruction and fatigue can reduce
useful alveolar ventilation.

28
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:22,584
Ask about usual exercise tolerance, saturation, oxygen and support.
The important story is how the current function differs, not whether
the patient matches a body-shape stereotype.

29
00:05:22,584 --> 00:05:34,560
Known COPD does not exclude acute coronary disease or pulmonary edema.
Chest discomfort may be absent, and breathlessness may be the dominant
cardiac symptom.

30
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:48,000
Sudden deterioration, unequal air entry, trauma or new pressure
changes should reopen the differential. Do not keep increasing
positive pressure without considering pneumothorax and perfusion.

31
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:58,368
Deep rapid breathing can be compensation for metabolic acidosis.
Suppressing it or assuming anxiety because the rate is high can worsen
the underlying problem.

32
00:05:58,368 --> 00:06:08,976
Check the equipment path as well as disease. A disconnected tube and a
severe illness can coexist; repairing one does not prove the other has
resolved.

33
00:06:08,976 --> 00:06:19,176
The checked COPD guidance generally targets eighty-eight to ninety-two
percent. Patient baseline and the actual clinical state provide
additional context.

34
00:06:19,176 --> 00:06:29,184
This is different from the school post-ROSC target of ninety-four to
ninety-eight. Targets are not interchangeable simply because both
involve breathlessness.

35
00:06:29,184 --> 00:06:41,880
Never deny needed oxygen or assisted ventilation because of a
simplistic hypoxic-drive explanation. Treat immediate hypoxemia while
monitoring the whole patient and titrating appropriately.

36
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:52,920
Saturation does not measure carbon-dioxide clearance. A rising oxygen
number with worsening fatigue needs a new ventilation decision, not
congratulations.

37
00:06:52,920 --> 00:07:03,984
Poor perfusion, movement, a poor probe fit or cold extremities can
compromise oximetry. Check signal quality and whether the displayed
pulse plausibly matches the patient.

38
00:07:03,984 --> 00:07:16,056
A reliable saturation still does not describe hemoglobin amount or
effective blood flow. An anemic or shocked patient can have inadequate
delivery despite an acceptable percentage.

39
00:07:16,056 --> 00:07:28,896
Capnography adds information about ventilation, perfusion and the
sampling path. A sudden change must be linked to the patient and
circuit rather than read as a stand-alone diagnosis.

40
00:07:28,896 --> 00:07:37,944
Do not postpone necessary support while trying to perfect an
instrument reading. Treat the credible clinical threat while
troubleshooting safely.

41
00:07:37,944 --> 00:07:49,104
With obstruction, exhalation may remain incomplete when the next
breath begins. Repeated excessive breaths can progressively increase
trapped volume and pressure.

42
00:07:49,104 --> 00:07:58,632
Raised intrathoracic pressure can impair venous return, lower pressure
and make further ventilation difficult. Barotrauma is another concern.

43
00:07:58,632 --> 00:08:12,336
Use effective airway positioning, seal and appropriate assistance,
allowing exhalation and reassessing response. More force and a faster
rate are not automatic answers to every poor oxygen number.

44
00:08:12,336 --> 00:08:24,480
The rate depends on age, spontaneous effort, arrest state and the
authorized pathway. Do not copy an isolated old textbook rate as a
universal rule for severe COPD.

45
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:35,256
When breathing is inadequate, support ventilation promptly through the
appropriate airway pathway. Do not wait for a steroid, a gas result or
a better CPAP fit.

46
00:08:35,256 --> 00:08:45,720
Position the airway, clear obstruction and use indicated adjuncts. A
second trained provider can help maintain the seal and airway while
the other manages the bag.

47
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:57,072
Deliver effective breaths with visible chest movement, without
excessive volume or rate. Check that the chest can fall and air can
leave, particularly in obstruction.

48
00:08:57,072 --> 00:09:09,048
Reassess oxygen supply, connections, air entry and circulation. An
added valve cannot repair an obstructed airway, empty cylinder or poor
basic ventilation.

49
00:09:09,048 --> 00:09:20,328
Salbutamol relaxes bronchial smooth muscle through beta-two
stimulation. Its effect is relevant when bronchospasm contributes, not
every cause of breathlessness.

50
00:09:20,328 --> 00:09:32,400
Ipratropium reduces muscarinic bronchoconstrictor activity and
complements salbutamol. The local acute regimen uses one dose rather
than an automatic dose with every repeat.

51
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:44,304
Dexamethasone addresses inflammation over hours. A patient in
immediate ventilatory failure needs support now, not observation until
the anti-inflammatory effect peaks.

52
00:09:44,304 --> 00:09:56,256
Mechanism guides the reassessment: ask whether air movement, effort,
symptoms and perfusion improved, and whether an adverse effect or
alternative diagnosis is emerging.

53
00:09:56,256 --> 00:10:06,960
Confirm the medicine and strength, expiry and remaining doses.
Propellant or a shaking sound alone does not prove useful medicine
remains.

54
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:17,544
Prepare the actual inhaler and spacer as instructed. Check the
mouthpiece and connection, and explain the inhalation technique before
dispensing the dose.

55
00:10:17,544 --> 00:10:27,744
Use one actuation at a time with the device-specific inhalation
method. Do not discharge several puffs together or leave a prepared
cloud waiting for minutes.

56
00:10:27,744 --> 00:10:39,720
Observe whether the patient can actually inhale the dose and reassess
response. If effort or consciousness fails, change the support plan
rather than repeating an ineffective ritual.

57
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:51,960
Check the product, dose and units before adding medication. Micrograms
and milligrams are not interchangeable, and premixed products must be
identified rather than guessed.

58
00:10:51,960 --> 00:11:03,288
Use the actual nebulizer operating flow and a suitable patient
interface. Mist escaping while the patient turns away does not
establish the intended lung dose.

59
00:11:03,288 --> 00:11:15,720
Apply indicated infection-control precautions. An MDI with spacer is
preferred in some infectious circumstances, but the choice still
depends on appropriate delivery and the actual pathway.

60
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:26,424
Monitor oxygenation and ventilation throughout treatment. The
nebulizer label does not guarantee a universal inspired oxygen
percentage or adequate breathing.

61
00:11:26,424 --> 00:11:37,752
The current adult monograph lists five milligrams nebulized, repeated
back-to-back as needed, or four one-hundred-microgram actuations with
spacer, repeated as needed.

62
00:11:37,752 --> 00:11:48,960
The route-specific dose must match the actual product and authorized
indication. Do not substitute the chronic home frequency or an adult
dose for a pediatric calculation.

63
00:11:48,960 --> 00:12:01,776
Look for improved air movement and reduced effort, but also tremor,
tachycardia, dysrhythmia or paradoxical worsening. Beta-two
selectivity does not mean zero systemic effect.

64
00:12:01,776 --> 00:12:13,776
High-dose nebulized treatment for hyperkalemia is separately listed at
ACP level. It is not a second PCP bronchospasm dose available just
because the medication is familiar.

65
00:12:13,776 --> 00:12:24,600
The checked adult local dose is five hundred micrograms nebulized, or
eight twenty-microgram actuations by MDI, totaling one hundred sixty
micrograms.

66
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:34,584
It is an adjunct single dose. Salbutamol may be repeated as indicated,
but this does not authorize repeated ipratropium with each cycle.

67
00:12:34,584 --> 00:12:44,808
Pediatric dose and consultation requirements are different, and the
source excludes neonates under thirty days. Do not let the adult card
erase those gates.

68
00:12:44,808 --> 00:12:57,000
Protect the eyes and consider relevant glaucoma and urinary-retention
cautions. Report actual response or paradoxical worsening rather than
assuming more medicine is always needed.

69
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:10,224
The checked monograph considers dexamethasone for known asthma or COPD
with inadequate improvement after salbutamol and ipratropium. Confirm
the actual indication, not wheeze alone.

70
00:13:10,224 --> 00:13:24,168
Adult PCP dosing is eight milligrams intravenously or orally, with
oral preferred and prior CliniCall required. Verify the vial
concentration rather than assuming all stock has one strength.

71
00:13:24,168 --> 00:13:36,648
Clinical effect is delayed: up to two hours intravenously and two to
six hours orally in the monograph. Continue necessary respiratory
support while the drug has not yet acted.

72
00:13:36,648 --> 00:13:50,592
Check contraindications, cautions and the patient context. A steroid
does not make CPAP safe for a failing airway, and a suspected
infection is not a blanket ban on every COPD steroid plan.

73
00:13:50,592 --> 00:14:03,792
Severe obstruction with exhaustion, deteriorating consciousness or
very poor air entry requires urgent escalation. Do not wait for
saturation to become extreme before recognizing failing function.

74
00:14:03,792 --> 00:14:13,944
Continue effective airway and ventilation support while arranging
appropriate help and conveyance. Avoid unnecessary exertion or lengthy
testing.

75
00:14:13,944 --> 00:14:46,584
For selected severe asthma or impending respiratory arrest, the
checked adult PCP epinephrine entry is zero point five milligrams
intramuscularly every five to twenty minutes, with prior CliniCall
recommended. Verify the actual indication and product concentration.
This is not the anaphylaxis repeat schedule or an intravenous arrest
dose. Pediatric weight-based dosing is separately stated in the
bilingual reading; the adult repeat interval is not automatically
imported.

76
00:14:46,584 --> 00:14:59,208
IV epinephrine, magnesium and intubation are different advanced
actions. Understanding why they may be used does not grant a PCP a new
medication or airway permission.

77
00:14:59,208 --> 00:15:11,568
Pulmonary edema impairs gas exchange and increases respiratory work.
Ask whether a failing pump or another process explains the
presentation, and check pressure and perfusion.

78
00:15:11,568 --> 00:15:21,816
A tolerated upright position can help an appropriate patient, but do
not impose it on an unconscious or shocked person regardless of airway
and circulation needs.

79
00:15:21,816 --> 00:15:48,360
Selected CPAP can recruit lung and reduce work. C06 places pulmonary-
edema nitroglycerin under advanced care paramedic interventions. A PCP
must not create a new pulmonary-edema nitrate order merely by passing
the separate acute-coronary-syndrome pressure screen. Indication,
role, exclusions and consultation all matter; crackles alone are not
enough.

80
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:58,896
Do not copy an older automatic fluid, furosemide or morphine list.
Mechanism, current physiology, role and response determine the safe
pathway.

81
00:15:58,896 --> 00:16:08,544
First ask whether spontaneous breathing is adequate enough for a
noninvasive CPAP strategy. An oxygen deficit alone does not answer
that question.

82
00:16:08,544 --> 00:16:18,384
When breathing is inadequate, use effective assisted ventilation
through the appropriate airway pathway. CPAP does not provide missing
rescue breaths.

83
00:16:18,384 --> 00:16:28,512
If hypoxemia persists despite good bag-mask technique and an
appropriate airway, consider the separate PEEP procedure only after
its own exclusions are checked.

84
00:16:28,512 --> 00:16:38,856
Every branch includes repeated assessment and a stop plan. A device
selected ten minutes ago is not guaranteed to be correct after the
patient changes.

85
00:16:38,856 --> 00:16:49,032
CPAP maintains positive airway pressure while the patient breathes
spontaneously. It can keep more alveoli available and reduce the work
of a selected patient.

86
00:16:49,032 --> 00:17:00,720
It is not the same as bilevel pressure support, and it does not
deliver mandatory breaths to an apneic person. Names containing
positive pressure are not interchangeable modes.

87
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:10,968
Positive pressure can reduce venous return and blood pressure. A
treatment that helps oxygenation may become unsafe if circulation
deteriorates.

88
00:17:10,968 --> 00:17:21,864
Explain the intended benefit and the risk before fitting the mask. A
successful procedure includes physiological benefit and tolerability,
not merely a tight seal.

89
00:17:21,864 --> 00:17:30,432
The checked entry screen requires a patient at least thirteen years
old who is awake, follows commands and can maintain an open airway.

90
00:17:30,432 --> 00:17:43,752
It also lists all three respiratory findings: rate above twenty-four,
saturation below ninety-four percent on oxygen and accessory-muscle
use. Any one finding alone is not the full screen.

91
00:17:43,752 --> 00:17:56,904
Check the actual indication and operator training requirement. Prior
CliniCall is recommended in PR09; that recommendation is distinct from
a mandatory medication consultation gate.

92
00:17:56,904 --> 00:18:07,656
Eligibility is only the beginning. The contraindication screen,
patient consent, equipment and reassessment plan must also be
satisfactory before starting.

93
00:18:07,656 --> 00:18:19,584
Exclude apnea, hypoventilation and impending failure with slowing
shallow effort or falling consciousness. CPAP is not a substitute for
effective assisted ventilation.

94
00:18:19,584 --> 00:18:31,584
Vomiting or aspiration risk and inability to protect the airway can
make a strapped mask unsafe. Prepare the alternative rather than
hoping a better oxygen reading will cancel the risk.

95
00:18:31,584 --> 00:18:42,792
The listed exclusions include systolic pressure below ninety,
suspected pneumothorax, traumatic respiratory distress, tracheostomy
and inability to obtain a proper fit.

96
00:18:42,792 --> 00:18:54,360
Age and ability to follow commands remain relevant. Repeat the screen
as the patient changes; absence of a contraindication at the start
does not guarantee continued safety.

97
00:18:54,360 --> 00:19:05,256
Explain what the mask will feel like, obtain appropriate consent and
choose a tolerated position. Record baseline respiratory function,
pressure and perfusion.

98
00:19:05,256 --> 00:19:17,328
Identify the actual kit and check its assembly instructions,
connections, valve, mask and pressure-flow chart. Do not transfer a
remembered flow setting from another device.

99
00:19:17,328 --> 00:19:27,912
Estimate oxygen availability for treatment and transport, including
anticipated flow changes. A plan that exhausts the cylinder before
arrival is incomplete.

100
00:19:27,912 --> 00:19:38,784
Have suction, effective bag-mask support, monitoring and a clear
escalation plan ready. Assign who will manage the airway if CPAP must
be removed immediately.

101
00:19:38,784 --> 00:19:51,288
Start with the procedure-specified low setup for the actual device.
PR09 describes five liters per minute if possible, otherwise six; the
generated pressure depends on the equipment.

102
00:19:51,288 --> 00:20:03,984
Let the awake patient hold the mask initially when feasible, then
progress to the indicated securing method. Coaching and consent can
improve tolerance more safely than immediate force.

103
00:20:03,984 --> 00:20:14,928
Check fit and leaks without over-tightening. Follow the actual bonnet
or strap design rather than improvising a way that creates pressure
injury or blocks access.

104
00:20:14,928 --> 00:20:26,400
Immediately reassess breathing, responsiveness, saturation, pressure
and perfusion. If the patient cannot tolerate or safely use the
interface, change the plan.

105
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:38,712
The saved examination sequence begins at five centimeters of water
pressure. Use the actual manufacturer relationship between flow and
pressure rather than equating liters with centimeters.

106
00:20:38,712 --> 00:20:51,720
The current PR09 escalation wording uses saturation remaining below
ninety-two percent. That differs from the entry criterion below
ninety-four on oxygen; keep the two decisions distinct.

107
00:20:51,720 --> 00:21:03,432
When indicated, titrate through the device chart in the stated
pressure steps, reassessing after changes. Flow adjustments may change
both pressure and delivered oxygen.

108
00:21:03,432 --> 00:21:12,600
The treatment maximum is ten centimeters of water. Higher settings
printed on an equipment table do not authorize exceeding the clinical
ceiling.

109
00:21:12,600 --> 00:21:23,256
Assess air movement and work, not just saturation. The intended
outcome is improved effective function without new fatigue or loss of
airway protection.

110
00:21:23,256 --> 00:21:33,912
Continue to check responsiveness and ability to follow commands. New
drowsiness can move the patient out of the CPAP branch even if the
mask remains well sealed.

111
00:21:33,912 --> 00:21:45,048
Repeat pressure and perfusion assessment because positive pressure can
reduce preload. Do not accept a rising oxygen number as permission to
ignore falling circulation.

112
00:21:45,048 --> 00:21:56,328
Trace the device and oxygen path, preserving access to the patient.
Document actual observations and settings so the next team can
understand both benefit and risk.

113
00:21:56,328 --> 00:22:06,960
Deteriorating breathing, consciousness, pressure or airway protection
requires immediate reassessment. Do not tighten the mask and wait for
the number to recover.

114
00:22:06,960 --> 00:22:17,688
Remove an unsafe CPAP interface and provide appropriate airway and
assisted-ventilation support. Keep suction and a trained second
provider available where possible.

115
00:22:17,688 --> 00:22:27,480
PEEP is a separate choice with separate exclusions. A patient failing
CPAP does not automatically qualify for every form of additional
positive pressure.

116
00:22:27,480 --> 00:22:39,576
Escalate and communicate the actual change, previous settings, stop
time and current support. Preserve the sequence so a harmful treatment
is not inadvertently restarted.

117
00:22:39,576 --> 00:22:52,008
PR10 considers PEEP when saturation remains below ninety despite good
bag-mask technique and an appropriate airway. Fix basic ventilation
problems before assuming more pressure is needed.

118
00:22:52,008 --> 00:23:07,992
Screen the full exclusions, including cardiac arrest, suspected
pneumothorax, traumatic respiratory arrest and the specified pressure
limits. The exact-age-twelve wording gap requires clarification, not
an invented pediatric rule.

119
00:23:07,992 --> 00:23:20,616
Use the proper exhaust valve, start at five and increase by two point
five if indicated, to no more than ten. Maintain effective seal,
appropriate ventilation and time for exhalation.

120
00:23:20,616 --> 00:23:34,344
Monitor pressure and oxygenation; remove for the specified
hypotension, a new contraindication or equipment issue. CliniCall is
required when hypoxemia persists despite maximal oxygen.

121
00:23:34,344 --> 00:23:44,304
The entry and escalation thresholds serve different questions. Do not
replace both with a new halfway number or select whichever permits the
intervention you want.

122
00:23:44,304 --> 00:23:57,744
PR10 excludes older-than-twelve patients at systolic pressure at or
below ninety, while its removal sentence says below ninety. Record the
wording difference and do not invent a permissive loophole.

123
00:23:57,744 --> 00:24:07,608
The school oxygen target, COPD target and resuscitation targets also
have different contexts. State the named pathway whenever using a
number.

124
00:24:07,608 --> 00:24:20,664
If school device instructions or an assessed rubric are missing,
preserve that gap. A precise theory rehearsal is useful, but it is not
evidence that an unseen assembly step was taught correctly.

125
00:24:20,664 --> 00:25:04,536
Use WHIPS to focus the severe-bronchospasm history: worst episode,
hospital visits, ICU stays, puffer requirements and steroid
requirements. Ask questions answerable with a word or head movement
while support continues. That history helps anticipate deterioration
rather than merely assigning a severity label. Salbutamol may briefly
provoke cough or louder sounds; distinguish this from falling air
entry, exhaustion or reduced awareness. A rapidly worsening patient
needs immediate reassessment and support, not a rule to wait until
every nebulizer finishes. Follow the indicated refusal process if
conveyance is declined.

126
00:25:04,536 --> 00:25:46,296
The additional PR09 notes matter: never use a CPAP mask as a bag-mask
ventilation interface, and do not delay indicated medication just to
apply CPAP. An initial transient saturation fall does not justify
ignoring clinical deterioration. The source names an extra 7-8 L/min
when its connected nebulizer arrangement is used; this is accessory-
specific guidance, not a universal pressure conversion or permission
to exceed the pressure ceiling. Verify the actual kit and gas supply.
A DNR or MOST does not by itself rule out CPAP for symptom relief;
reconcile goals, consent and eligibility.

127
00:25:46,296 --> 00:26:00,456
At a cycling rest area, a fifty-six-year-old with known obstructive
disease is breathless with wheeze and marked accessory effort. Start
the primary assessment and appropriate treatment while obtaining a
brief history.

128
00:26:00,456 --> 00:26:12,792
After oxygen, saturation rises, but the patient becomes drowsier,
takes shallower breaths and has less audible air entry. Those changes
do not demonstrate recovery.

129
00:26:12,792 --> 00:26:24,360
Name the threat as failing effective ventilation and possible
exhaustion, while considering competing causes. Recheck airway,
circulation and actual treatment delivery.

130
00:26:24,360 --> 00:26:35,184
Provide appropriate assisted support and escalation rather than
applying CPAP to someone who no longer meets the functional screen. Do
not wait for a confirmatory blood gas.

131
00:26:35,184 --> 00:26:49,392
A seventy-four-year-old at a community workshop is awake with severe
work, wet lung sounds and high initial pressure. Pulmonary edema is a
leading concern, but the full assessment and differential still
matter.

132
00:26:49,392 --> 00:27:00,696
Assume the complete CPAP screen is met and the actual trained device
is used. Record the initial state, settings and treatment times while
primary care continues.

133
00:27:00,696 --> 00:27:09,816
Later the saturation is better, but pressure and responsiveness fall.
A benefit in one variable does not make the overall treatment safe.

134
00:27:09,816 --> 00:27:24,312
Reassess the patient, device, pressure effect and alternative causes,
stop unsafe support and use the appropriate rescue pathway. Report
what changed rather than describing CPAP as successful from saturation
alone.

135
00:27:24,312 --> 00:27:36,528
If support appears to fail, look at the patient first and provide
necessary rescue ventilation or oxygen. A prolonged equipment
investigation must not leave the patient unsupported.

136
00:27:36,528 --> 00:27:46,944
Trace the oxygen source, regulator, set flow and available reserve. A
sound of gas or a previously full cylinder is not a current supply
check.

137
00:27:46,944 --> 00:27:58,968
Follow the circuit to the patient, checking connections, valves, leaks
and obstruction using the actual instructions. Do not improvise an
unverified valve arrangement under pressure.

138
00:27:58,968 --> 00:28:10,680
After a correction, verify both equipment function and clinical
response. Document the fault and action, and arrange appropriate
equipment follow-up without losing patient care.

139
00:28:10,680 --> 00:28:22,512
Begin a rehearsal by naming the current threat, leading mechanism and
dangerous alternative. State the indication and every relevant
exclusion before touching the pressure device.

140
00:28:22,512 --> 00:28:37,248
Identify actual equipment, oxygen supply and monitoring, then
verbalize the starting setting, maximum and rescue plan. For medicine,
state product, route, dose, unit and consultation gate.

141
00:28:37,248 --> 00:28:48,264
After the intervention, say what improved, what could worsen and what
else the presentation could be. Use observed function instead of a
declaration that the procedure is done.

142
00:28:48,264 --> 00:28:59,712
Rehearse a deterioration and stop the unsafe branch promptly. This
prepares the reasoning for the school station; it does not substitute
for observed hands-on device performance.

143
00:28:59,712 --> 00:29:09,672
Start with who the patient is and why the respiratory problem is
urgent. Give the baseline and current function, not just an asthma or
COPD label.

144
00:29:09,672 --> 00:29:19,656
Report actual drug doses, routes and times, and the device, settings
and pressure changes. Distinguish what was administered from what was
only considered.

145
00:29:19,656 --> 00:29:32,592
Describe air movement, work, responsiveness, oxygenation and perfusion
before and after treatment. Include any deterioration and why support
was stopped or changed.

146
00:29:32,592 --> 00:29:44,832
End with current support, the next capability needed and a confirmed
receiving plan. Preserve uncertainty honestly so the next team does
not inherit a false final diagnosis.

147
00:29:44,832 --> 00:29:54,192
You should now explain why obstruction can cause air trapping and why
saturation, rate and sound alone cannot establish adequate
ventilation.

148
00:29:54,192 --> 00:30:05,160
Demonstrate the reasoning behind effective inhaled delivery and the
checked local adult regimens. Keep pediatric, consultation and
advanced-role differences visible.

149
00:30:05,160 --> 00:30:14,232
Reconstruct the complete CPAP screen, device-linked titration and stop
plan, then explain the separate PEEP indication and exclusions.

150
00:30:14,232 --> 00:30:26,280
Use the bilingual reading and original cases to practise decisions,
not recite fragments. The next workshop applies the same quality-first
reasoning to complex twelve-lead findings.
